# Limitations and Assumptions

This project is a portfolio piece built to demonstrate operational control concepts, not a
production system. This document is an honest account of where it simplifies reality, so that
anyone reviewing it — including in an interview — understands exactly what it does and doesn't
represent.

## Data

- **All data is synthetic.** Every instrument, price, corporate action, event, and settlement
  record was generated programmatically with a fixed random seed. No real customer, employer, or
  proprietary data was used at any point.
- **Data volumes are small by design.** 7 instruments, 42 prices, 5 corporate actions, and a
  handful of events/settlements — enough to exercise every control rule at least once, but far
  below the scale of a real broker's daily data volumes.
- **Data quality issues were deliberately embedded**, not naturally occurring. This means the
  detection rate on this dataset is not representative of real-world exception rates; it was
  designed so the controls would have something concrete to catch and demonstrate.

## SLA and Business Logic

- **The settlement SLA (2 days past expected settlement) is a simplified placeholder.** Real
  settlement SLAs vary by market, instrument type, and settlement cycle (e.g. T+1, T+2) and would
  need to be configured per instrument/market in a production system, not hardcoded as a single
  threshold.
- **Reconciliation matching is done on instrument + event type**, a simplified key. A production
  system would need more robust matching logic (e.g. handling partial matches, multiple events of
  the same type on the same day, corrections/amendments).
- **Severity and owner-team assignment are static, rule-based mappings.** A real system would
  likely route based on additional context (e.g. transaction size, customer impact) and would
  integrate with an actual ticketing/workflow system rather than a CSV.

## Technology

- **SQLite, not a production database.** Chosen for portability and simplicity in a demonstration
  project. A production deployment would use a proper RDBMS or data warehouse, with connection
  pooling, access controls, and backup/recovery — none of which are needed or implemented here.
- **No automation or scheduling yet.** Currently, every control script is run manually. A
  production system would run on a schedule (e.g. via GitHub Actions, Airflow, or a dedicated
  scheduler) and alert on failures, not just log them.
- **No user interface.** Exceptions and KPIs are currently CSV outputs, reviewed manually. A
  production system would likely have a dashboard or integrate with existing operational tooling.

## What a Production System Would Add

- Real-time or near-real-time processing rather than batch runs
- Configurable, market-specific SLA and business rules rather than hardcoded thresholds
- Integration with an actual case-management/ticketing system for exception workflow
- Proper access controls, audit logging, and data retention policies
- Monitoring and alerting on control run failures themselves (not just the exceptions they find)
- Scale-tested performance across realistic daily data volumes